@@ -106,6 +106,8 @@ A velocity file can be followed by the name of a **JSON file**, as written by [`
 
 The `inversion` entry (`default`, `push` or `newton`, as in [`register --inversion`](../registration/register.md#choosing-how-the-warp-is-inverted-with-inversion)) selects how inverted maps are computed. A JSON file without it behaves as before, with `default`.
 
+The `fast` entry, written by `register` (`false` with [`@nonlin shoot --exact`](../registration/register.md#fast-or-exact-shooting-with-exact)), selects the integration scheme: fast, which chains one-step transports, or exact, which converges to the geodesic as the number of steps grows. A velocity must be shot with the scheme it was estimated with. A JSON file without the entry uses the fast scheme.
+
 If you give no JSON file and a file with the same name as the velocity field and the extension `.json` exists, it is used automatically, whether it describes a shooting velocity (it contains a `bending` entry, or `"model": "shoot"`) or a stationary one (`"model": "svf"`). Otherwise the field is a stationary velocity field.
 
 **Time bounds.** A velocity field describes a path of transformations indexed by time. By convention, the moving image sits at time 0 and the fixed image at time 1. The tag `-v FILE T0 T1` applies the part of that path that goes from time `T0` to time `T1`: it turns an image at time `T0` into the image at time `T1`. The bounds default to 0 and 1, so `-v FILE 0 1` is the same as `-v FILE`. For a stationary velocity field, the transformation is exactly $\exp\big((T_1 - T_0)\,v\big)$. For a shooting velocity (one with a JSON file), the field is integrated by geodesic shooting over the interval from `T0` to `T1`. Bounds outside $[0, 1]$ are allowed and extrapolate the path, with the same loss of accuracy as for larger velocities.
diff --git a/docs/cli/reference/spatial/vexp.md b/docs/cli/reference/spatial/vexp.md
index 48212742..1d8cbc98 100644
--- a/docs/cli/reference/spatial/vexp.md
+++ b/docs/cli/reference/spatial/vexp.md
@@ -17,6 +17,8 @@ Exponentiate a stationary velocity field, by scaling and squaring, into a dense
 
 A *stationary velocity field* $v(x)$ is a vector field that does not change with time. Following the flow of $v$ for one unit of time, that is, solving $\dot\phi_t = v(\phi_t)$ with $\phi_0 = \mathrm{id}$, yields a transformation $\phi_1 = \exp(v)$. Because it is the end point of a smooth flow, this transformation is invertible and has an invertible, smooth inverse (it is a *diffeomorphism*): $\exp(v)^{-1} = \exp(-v)$. This is why registration methods such as the one in [`register`](../registration/register.md) optimise a velocity field rather than a displacement field directly.
 
+`vexp` only exponentiates stationary velocity fields. The initial velocity written by `register @nonlin shoot` is integrated by geodesic shooting instead; use [`reslice`](reslice.md#velocity-fields) or [`compose`](compose.md) for it.
+
 `vexp` computes $\exp(v)$ with **scaling and squaring**. The velocity is first divided by $2^N$, which makes it small enough for $\exp(v/2^N) \approx \mathrm{id} + v/2^N$ to be accurate. The resulting transformation is then composed with itself $N$ times, each composition doubling the time that has been integrated, until the full unit of time is reached. $N$ is set with `-n` (default 8, so the flow is integrated in 256 small steps). More steps give a more accurate result and cost one more resampling of the field each.
 
 The velocity file must hold the three components of the vector in its last dimension (a volume of $X \times Y \times Z \times 3$ values), expressed in **voxels** of the lattice of the file itself. Each input file is processed independently; its orientation matrix is copied to the output.
